 happens at lower temperatures – that's why puddles dry up. But at 100°C, it happens so rapidly that we call it boiling, and that temperature is a very important physical constant." That would take 5 seconds and remove all confusion. But many professors fall into the trap of teaching the same way they were taught, without reflecting on potential misunderstandings. ### What this means for you You have now identified a real weakness in how science is often taught. The professor isn't trying to hide anything; they are just using a shorthand that works for most students but fails for thoughtful ones like you. You are not wrong to be confused – the confusion is a natural result of an incomplete explanation. ### What you can do If you feel comfortable, you can ask your professor directly in office hours: "I noticed you said water evaporates at 100°C, but I've seen puddles dry up at much lower temperatures. Could you help me understand how both statements are true?" A good professor will appreciate your critical thinking and give you a clear explanation. If they dismiss the question, that says more about them than about you. Either way, you now have a deeper understanding than most students – and that's a victory.
